"ch": 1,
"title": "Healing: Regeneration and Repair",
"summary": "How tissues respond to injury: regeneration vs repair by fibrosis, cell proliferative classes, stem cells, growth factors, the extracellular matrix, granulation tissue and angiogenesis, skin wound healing by first and second intention, bone fracture healing, and abnormal healing.",
"formulas": [
"Regeneration = same cell type replaces injured cells (needs intact ECM); repair = fibrous scar (ECM damaged or permanent cells)",
"Cell classes: labile (continuously dividing), stable/quiescent (G0, can re-enter G1), permanent (neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle)",
"Granulation tissue = new vessels (VEGF) + fibroblasts/myofibroblasts + inflammatory cells; template for the scar",
"First intention: sutured clean wound; second intention: large defect, more granulation, wound contraction by myofibroblasts",
"Keloid grows beyond the wound margins; hypertrophic scar stays within them"
],
"sections": [
{
"h": "Regeneration, repair and cell types",
"items": [
"Healing is the tissue response to injury or inflammation. Regeneration replaces injured cells with the same type and needs an intact extracellular matrix (liver, kidney, GI mucosa).",
"Repair by fibrosis replaces damaged tissue with fibrous tissue when the ECM framework is damaged or cells cannot divide (permanent cells), e.g. chronic inflammation.",
"Steps of repair: removal of debris (macrophages) → granulation tissue → scarring (collagen from fibroblasts, remodelling, contraction).",
"Labile cells divide continuously (skin epidermis, GIT and GU surface epithelium, bone-marrow haemopoietic cells). Stable cells are quiescent in G0 but re-enter G1 (hepatocytes, renal tubular cells, glandular cells, osteoblasts, endothelium). Permanent cells have left the cycle (neurons, skeletal and cardiac muscle).",
"Liver: single large CCl4 dose, ECM intact, regeneration; repeated small doses, ECM damaged, fibrosis. Renal tubule: intact basement membrane needed for organised regeneration.",
"Fibrosis in chronic inflammation: persistent stimulus → activated macrophages/lymphocytes → growth factors (PDGF, FGF, TGF-β) and cytokines (TNF, IL-1, IL-4, IL-13) → fibroblast proliferation, more collagen synthesis, less collagen degradation (decreased metalloproteinase activity)."
]
},
{
"h": "Stem cells, growth factors and ECM",
"items": [
"Stem cells: self-renewal, differentiation, plasticity. Types: embryonic (pluripotent), haemopoietic, marrow stromal, adult (in niches), induced pluripotent (iPS) by gene reprogramming.",
"Growth factors are polypeptides acting by autocrine, paracrine or endocrine signalling; sources include macrophages, platelets, T cells, fibroblasts, endothelium.",
"EGF: mitogenic for keratinocytes and fibroblasts, promotes granulation tissue. TGF-α: epithelial and hepatocyte proliferation. HGF: hepatocytes. VEGF: endothelial proliferation, increased vascular permeability. PDGF: chemotactic and mitogenic for fibroblasts and smooth muscle. FGF: fibroblast mitogen, angiogenesis. TGF-β: stimulates ECM synthesis, suppresses acute inflammation. KGF (FGF-7): keratinocyte proliferation.",
"ECM: interstitial matrix (collagens, glycoproteins), basement membranes (non-fibrillar collagen, laminin), adhesive glycoproteins (cadherins, integrins), proteoglycans, hyaluronan. Functions: mechanical support (collagen, elastin), substrate for growth, regulation of proliferation, proteoglycans store growth factors, fibronectin and laminin signal through integrins."
]
},
{
"h": "Granulation tissue and wound healing",
"items": [
"Granulation tissue (Latin granum = grain) is red, granular, soft and moist: new vessels (angiogenesis), proliferating fibroblasts and myofibroblasts, inflammatory cells.",
"Angiogenesis: stimulated by VEGF; angiopoietins and PDGF recruit pericytes and smooth muscle; TGF-β stabilises vessels via ECM; sources are pre-existing vessels and endothelial precursor cells from marrow.",
"Fibroblast proliferation is driven by PDGF, FGF, TGF-β, TNF; remodelling by matrix metalloproteinases (collagenases), restrained by tissue inhibitors (TIMPs).",
"Organisation: healing through granulation tissue in abscess, infarct, thrombus, fibrin and haematoma.",
"Three phases: inflammatory (clot, chemotaxis) → proliferative (re-epithelialisation, angiogenesis, granulation tissue) → maturation (collagen deposition, wound contraction).",
"First intention (sutured surgical wound): minutes clot/scab; 24 h neutrophils and epithelial cells; day 3 granulation tissue; day 5–7 incision filled, collagen bridges; day 7 about 10 % tensile strength; 2 weeks fibroblasts and collagen; 4+ weeks pale avascular scar; sutured wound reaches ~70 % of normal strength.",
"Second intention: large defect with distant edges (graft may be needed), longer, more intense inflammation, abundant granulation, wound contraction by myofibroblasts (smooth-muscle α-actin, vimentin), maximum 0.75 mm/day.",
"Wound strength: type III collagen replaced by type I; cross-linking increases tensile strength.",
"Fetal skin wounds heal without scar (TGF-β isoforms, no osteopontin, no TH2 response); skin appendages do not regenerate."
]
},
{
"h": "Bone, organs and abnormal healing",
"items": [
"Simple fracture: skin intact; compound: skin broken with a high infection risk. Indirect healing: haematoma → macrophages/osteoclasts clean → osteogenic granulation tissue (soft callus, 1–6 weeks) → osteoid → woven bone (hard callus) → remodelling to lamellar bone (8+ weeks; years).",
"Abnormal bone healing: delayed union, non-union (pseudoarthrosis), malunion, refracture.",
"Organs: epithelia regenerate; liver regenerates if ECM intact, fibrosis/cirrhosis if destroyed; cardiac muscle heals by fibrosis (scar after MI); neurons by gliosis.",
"Abnormal healing: deficient scar (ulcers from poor perfusion or neuropathy, dehiscence 1–3 % after laparotomy at 7–10 days), excess repair (proud flesh, hypertrophic scar, keloid), contracture (burns), atrophic scar, implantation cyst, pigment changes.",
"Keloid: grows beyond the wound, does not regress, more common in black people, earlobes/shoulders/upper back/chest. Hypertrophic scar stays within the wound and may regress.",
"Local factors: drying/maceration, infection, necrosis (slough, eschar), pressure, oedema, foreign bodies, location, radiation. Systemic: age, protein, vitamin C and zinc deficiency, vascular insufficiency, steroids, diabetes, obesity, immunosuppression, smoking."
]
}
],
"examples": [],
"tips": [
"Know the day-by-day sequence of first-intention healing.",
"Vitamin C is needed for collagen cross-linking; steroids and diabetes impair healing."
]
