"sections": [
{
"h": "Nomenclature and classification",
"items": [
"Neoplasia = 'new growth'. Oncology = study of tumours. Benign: localised, cannot spread, amenable to surgery. Malignant (cancer, Latin 'crab'): invades, destroys, metastasises.",
"Components: parenchyma (proliferating neoplastic cells; determines behaviour and name) and stroma (connective tissue and vessels). Desmoplasia = abundant collagenous stroma.",
"Mesenchymal: benign -oma, malignant -sarcoma (fibroma/fibrosarcoma, lipoma/liposarcoma, chondroma/chondrosarcoma, osteoma/osteogenic sarcoma, haemangioma/angiosarcoma, leiomyoma/leiomyosarcoma, rhabdomyoma/rhabdomyosarcoma).",
"Epithelial: adenoma (glandular), papilloma (finger-like projections), cystadenoma, polyp (visible projection above a mucosa); malignant = carcinoma (squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma).",
"Special terms: mixed tumour (pleomorphic adenoma of salivary gland), teratoma (more than one germ layer from totipotent cells; ovarian dermoid cyst is benign mature), lymphoma (lymphoid), leukaemia (blood-forming cells), choristoma (ectopic normal tissue), hamartoma (disorganised mature tissue native to the site)."
]
},
{
"h": "Benign vs malignant",
"items": [
"Differentiation: how closely tumour cells resemble normal cells; benign are well differentiated; malignant range from well to anaplastic (anaplasia = 'form backward').",
"Anaplasia: pleomorphism, hyperchromatic nuclei with high N:C ratio (~1:1 vs normal 1:4–1:6), large nucleoli, abnormal mitoses (tripolar/quadripolar), loss of polarity, tumour giant cells, necrosis.",
"Dysplasia = disordered growth of epithelium; if full thickness but confined above the basement membrane it is carcinoma in situ; dysplasia does not necessarily progress.",
"Growth rate depends on doubling time, growth fraction (cells in the replicative pool) and cell loss. A cell needs ~30 doublings to make 10^9 cells (1 g, smallest detectable mass); at 3-day cell cycle that is ~90 days. Chemotherapy works best on cycling cells.",
"Invasion: benign tumours are expansile with a fibrous capsule (exceptions: haemangioma, neurofibroma); malignant tumours infiltrate and are poorly demarcated. Carcinoma in situ has not breached the basement membrane.",
"Metastasis (discontinuous implants) unequivocally marks malignancy; exceptions that rarely metastasise: gliomas and basal cell carcinoma.",
"Pathways: transcoelomic seeding (ovarian carcinoma, pseudomyxoma peritonei), lymphatic (commonest initial route for carcinoma, e.g. breast to axillary nodes), haematogenous (typical for sarcoma; liver and lung most often; renal cell carcinoma grows up renal vein into IVC; paravertebral plexus for thyroid and prostate)."
]
},
{
"h": "Epidemiology and predisposition",
"items": [
"Commonest tumours: men prostate, lung, colorectal; women breast, lung, colorectal, cervix.",
"Geography: stomach (Japan), lung (USA), skin (NZ/Australia), liver (Ethiopia). Environment: asbestos (lung, mesothelioma), vinyl chloride (liver angiosarcoma), smoking (90 % of lung cancer), alcohol, HPV and sexual history (cervix), obesity.",
"Age: most adult cancers after 55; children get leukaemia, CNS tumours, neuroblastoma, Wilms tumour, retinoblastoma, rhabdomyosarcoma.",
"Autosomal dominant syndromes: familial retinoblastoma (RB), familial adenomatous polyposis (APC, 100 % colon cancer by age 50), MEN, neurofibromatosis. Autosomal recessive DNA-repair defects: xeroderma pigmentosum, ataxia telangiectasia, Bloom syndrome. Familial cancers: sibling relative risk 2–3.",
"Acquired precursors: persistent regeneration (SCC in chronic fistula; HCC in cirrhosis), dysplasia (bronchial mucosa in smokers), leukoplakia, chronic inflammation (H. pylori → gastric carcinoma/lymphoma; ulcerative colitis; viral hepatitis → HCC; chronic pancreatitis), villous colonic adenoma."
]
},
{
"h": "Molecular basis of cancer",
"items": [
"Non-lethal genetic damage; tumours are monoclonal (X-linked G6PD evidence). Four classes of target genes: proto-oncogenes (growth promoting), tumour-suppressor genes (growth inhibiting), apoptosis genes, DNA-repair genes.",
"Cell cycle: G0, G1, S, G2, M; cyclin D–CDK4 phosphorylates RB, releasing E2F at the G1/S restriction point; cyclin E–CDK2 for S phase; cyclin A–CDK2 for G2/M; cyclin B–CDK1 breaks down nuclear envelope. CDK inhibitors (p21, induced by p53). Checkpoints: G1/S and G2/M (ATM senses damage, p53 effector).",
"Oncogenes act through growth factors (autocrine), receptors (mutation, overexpression: EGFR family in breast cancer), signal transducers (RAS: 15–20 % of tumours, loses GTPase activity so stays GTP-bound; RAF–MAPK cascade), transcription factors (MYC; Burkitt lymphoma t(8;14)).",
"RB: first tumour-suppressor gene; two hits needed; familial 40 % (born with one defective allele), sporadic 60 %; carriers at risk of osteosarcoma. Hypophosphorylated RB binds E2F.",
"p53: 17p13.1, mutated in >50 % of cancers; causes G1 arrest, induces DNA repair genes, triggers apoptosis if damage irreparable; germline mutation = Li-Fraumeni syndrome (one hit needed).",
"Apoptosis evasion: BCL2 overexpression from t(14;18) in ~85 % of follicular B-cell lymphomas; indolent because of reduced death, not rapid proliferation.",
"DNA repair: mismatch repair (HNPCC, microsatellite instability), nucleotide excision repair (xeroderma pigmentosum, UV skin cancer), recombination repair (BRCA1/BRCA2: ~80 % of familial breast cancer).",
"Limitless replication: normal cells 60–70 doublings then senescence due to telomere shortening; tumours reactivate telomerase. Angiogenesis: VEGF, 'angiogenic switch', tumour vessels leaky and disorganised.",
"Invasion cascade: detachment (loss of E-cadherin), attachment to laminin/fibronectin, ECM degradation (serine, cysteine proteases, MMPs), migration; then intravasation, emboli with platelets, adhesion to endothelium, extravasation, homing to target organ."
]
},
{
"h": "Laboratory diagnosis",
"items": [
"Biopsy (incisional/excisional), fine-needle aspiration (less invasive, faster, cheaper), Pap smear (cervical in-situ cancer), fluid cytology, immunohistochemistry (e.g. oestrogen receptor → tamoxifen), molecular tests, flow cytometry (DNA content, aneuploidy).",
"Tumour markers are supportive only: hCG (trophoblastic, non-seminomatous testicular), calcitonin (medullary thyroid carcinoma), catecholamines (phaeochromocytoma), α-fetoprotein (HCC, germ-cell), CEA (colon, pancreas), PAP (prostate), CA-125 (ovary), immunoglobulins (myeloma)."
]
}
],
