"title": "Hypersensitivity Reactions",
"summary": "Definition and the four types of hypersensitivity: mediators, mechanism, timing, clinical examples, diagnosis and management, including emergency treatment of anaphylaxis.",
"formulas": [
"Type I: IgE + mast cells, immediate (minutes), anaphylaxis, allergic rhinitis, asthma",
"Type II: IgG/IgM against cell-surface or tissue antigens: AIHA, haemolytic disease of the newborn, Goodpasture, myasthenia gravis, Graves",
"Type III: immune complex deposition + complement + neutrophils: serum sickness, SLE, Arthus, post-strep GN",
"Type IV: T-cell mediated, delayed 24–72 h: contact dermatitis (nickel), tuberculin test, multiple sclerosis",
"Anaphylaxis: IM epinephrine into mid-anterolateral thigh first; do not delay for antihistamines"
],
"sections": [
{
"h": "Concepts and classification",
"items": [
"Hypersensitivity: exaggerated or inappropriate immune response to an antigen causing tissue damage. Autoimmunity: failure of self-tolerance, attack on own tissues.",
"Allergy: immune response to a usually harmless substance (pollen, food). IgE: key antibody in type I. Complement: C3a and C5a promote inflammation (C5a attracts leukocytes), C3b coats targets, C5b–C9 forms the membrane attack complex. T lymphocytes: major in type IV.",
"Type I immediate (IgE, mast cells); type II antibody-mediated (IgG/IgM vs cell or tissue antigens); type III immune-complex mediated; type IV delayed T-cell mediated (24–72 h)."
]
},
{
"h": "Type I (immediate)",
"items": [
"Sensitisation: allergen → Th2 cells → B cells make allergen-specific IgE → IgE binds high-affinity FcεRI on mast cells and basophils. Re-exposure: allergen cross-links IgE → degranulation.",
"Mediators: histamine (itching, vasodilation, leak, swelling), leukotrienes and prostaglandins (bronchoconstriction, mucus), cytokines (IL-4 and IL-13 drive IgE switching; IL-5 and eotaxin recruit eosinophils).",
"Phases: immediate within minutes; late phase 2–24 h with eosinophil infiltration and prolonged inflammation.",
"Examples: allergic rhinitis, allergic asthma, food allergy (peanuts), anaphylaxis.",
"Diagnosis: history, examination, skin-prick test, serum-specific IgE. Management: allergen avoidance, antihistamines, corticosteroids, bronchodilators, allergen immunotherapy.",
"Anaphylaxis: IM epinephrine in the mid-anterolateral thigh, repeat after 5–15 min if needed; α1 vasoconstriction and less mucosal oedema, β1 heart rate and contractility, β2 bronchodilation. Plus airway, oxygen, IV fluids, monitoring."
]
},
{
"h": "Types II and III",
"items": [
"Type II steps: antigen on cell or tissue → IgG/IgM bind → complement activation, phagocytosis, ADCC by NK cells → cell damage or dysfunction.",
"Cells: plasma cells, neutrophils, NK cells, macrophages. Mediators: complement (C3b, C5b–C9, C3a, C5a), enzymes, ROS.",
"Examples: autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (anaemia, jaundice), haemolytic disease of the newborn, Goodpasture (basement membrane of kidney and lung), myasthenia gravis (acetylcholine receptors: ptosis, weakness), Graves disease (stimulating TSH receptor antibodies).",
"Diagnosis: antibody tests, direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test, organ function tests, biopsy. Management: corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, plasma exchange, anti-D immunoglobulin.",
"Type III steps: antigen + IgG/IgM form immune complexes → deposit in vessel walls, kidneys, joints → complement activation (C3a, C5a) → neutrophils release enzymes and ROS → tissue damage (rash/purpura, arthralgia, vasculitis, kidney injury).",
"Examples: serum sickness, SLE, Arthus reaction (local), post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Diagnosis: low C3 and C4, antibodies, urine tests, biopsy. Management: treat cause, anti-inflammatory drugs, corticosteroids/immunosuppressants, supportive care."
]
},
{
"h": "Type IV (delayed)",
"items": [
"Sensitisation: antigen taken up by APCs and presented to T cells → memory T cells (usually no symptoms). Re-exposure: memory T cells release cytokines (IFN-γ activates macrophages), CD8+ cytotoxic cells kill target cells with perforin and granzymes.",
"Cells: Th1 (macrophage activation), Th17 (IL-17 recruits neutrophils), CD8+ T cells, macrophages. Mediators: IFN-γ, TNF, IL-17, perforin, granzymes.",
"Timing: 24–72 h after re-exposure.",
"Examples: contact dermatitis (nickel), tuberculin skin test, multiple sclerosis (CNS myelin). Diagnosis: history, patch testing."
]
}
],
"examples": [],
"tips": [
"Antibody-mediated = II and III; cell-mediated = IV; IgE = I.",
"Type II differs from III because antibodies bind fixed cell/tissue antigens rather than forming circulating complexes."
],
